---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>Blake Ledden</h1>
			<p>
				I write GPU kernels and run research experiments, and I publish what I find — including what
				didn't work. Current threads: quantum error correction decoders, portable GPU kernels
				(CUDA / ROCm / Metal), and LLM training dynamics — with an emphasis on statistical rigor
				(multi-seed validation, effect sizes, confidence intervals, paired tests).
			</p>

			<h2>Quantum Error Correction</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><a href="https://github.com/bledden/pathfinder">Pathfinder: Direction-Aware Neural Decoding and Complementary-Failure Ensembles for Surface Codes</a></strong> —
					a 57-page preprint. A single fixed-checkpoint CNN decoder is never statistically beaten by
					PyMatching across 24 evaluated configurations under circuit-level noise, and its ensemble
					beats a fine-tuned three-seed ensemble of the prior open-source GNN decoder under paired
					McNemar tests. Six negative results documented rather than buried. Code, checkpoints, and
					raw evaluation data in the repo.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><a href="https://github.com/bledden/tridec">tridec</a></strong> —
					vendor-portable GPU decoders for quantum LDPC codes: the same Triton kernel source runs on
					NVIDIA (CUDA), AMD (ROCm), and Apple silicon (Metal), validated on all three.
					<a href="https://pypi.org/project/tridec/">On PyPI</a>.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>GPU Kernels &amp; Consumer Blackwell</h2>
			<p>
				Written up on the <a href="https://getpoppy.app/blog/">Second Nature Computing blog</a>:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><a href="https://getpoppy.app/blog/four-months-upstreaming-consumer-blackwell">Four Months Upstreaming Consumer Blackwell</a></strong> —
					enabling SM120/SM121 across flash-attention, FlashInfer, vLLM, CUTLASS, Triton, and SGLang;
					merged forward/backward/varlen attention kernels and the dispatch fixes underneath them.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><a href="https://getpoppy.app/blog/writing-ptx-for-consumer-blackwell">Writing PTX for Consumer Blackwell</a></strong> —
					280 hand-written PTX kernels; FP8 flash attention from 30 to 108 TFLOPS via a transposed-V
					layout — and the honest counterpart: where hand-written PTX loses to the compiler.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>And on this site:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><a href="/blog/triton-msl">Same Source, Same Bytes</a></strong> —
					a Metal backend for OpenAI Triton with a correct-or-refuse contract: the same
					<code>@triton.jit</code> source, verified byte-identical against NVIDIA.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><a href="/blog/arrwdb-optimization">341× Faster</a></strong> —
					optimizing a Rust vector database to top-7 on ANN-Benchmarks.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>LLM Training Dynamics</h2>
			<p>
				A series of experiments on the <a href="https://thinkingmachines.ai/tinker/">Tinker platform</a>:
				<a href="/blog/noisy-student">Noisy Student for LLMs</a>,
				<a href="/blog/context-distillation">The Distribution Cliff</a>,
				<a href="/blog/open-character-training">Open Character Training</a>, and
				<a href="/blog/memorization-study">SL vs RL Efficiency</a> — synthesized in
				<a href="/blog/cross-project-meta-analysis">Seven Patterns from 300+ ML Evaluation Runs</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				<a href="/blog">See all posts →</a>
			</p>

			<h2>Now</h2>
			<p>
				Day to day I'm the founding AI engineer at <strong>Second Nature Computing</strong>, building
				<a href="https://getpoppy.app">Poppy</a> — a proactive assistant that surfaces the right thing
				at the right time across your calendar, mail, and messages. I'm also founder at
				<strong>Facilitair.ai</strong>, building AI orchestration systems.
			</p>

			<h2>Background</h2>
			<p>
				Previously 4+ years at Apple on authentication and developer tooling. Before that,
				I gravitated towards opportunities in startups and IT roles where I kept finding problems that could be automated.
			</p>

			<h2>Links</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><a href="https://github.com/bledden">GitHub</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://linkedin.com/in/blakeledden">LinkedIn</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://getpoppy.app/blog/">Second Nature blog</a></li>
				<li><a href="mailto:blake.ledden@gmail.com">blake.ledden@gmail.com</a></li>
			</ul>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
